WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.620
The crowd is roaring and the pressure is on.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.640
All Pro Basketball!

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:27.700
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:28.560
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.200
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:38.860
Today begs the serious question, just in the title alone, can it really be All Pro Basketball if the teams and players are fictitious?

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.420
They could be fictitious professionals though.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:50.000
Yeah, I guess I have no arguments about the concept of them being professionals in maybe, maybe that's the name of the league, right?

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.320
Like maybe it's the All Pro Basketball League.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:59.700
And, you know, and they're just the players of that league and we just don't know them because they're not from our universe.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:04.360
Yes. This is in the Watchmen universe actually.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.620
And inside that league, what the hell is going on with those team names?

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.300
You don't like the New York Slicks?

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.240
So the New York Slicks, right?

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:15.660
Funny wordplay.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.120
Right. I like that.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.160
Funny wordplay on New York Knicks.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.460
Then Chicago Zephyrs.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.480
No reference to Bulls at all in that.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.700
So not sure what the joke is there.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.320
Then the Boston Redcoats.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.660
The Redcoats were the guys who the Boston people were fighting.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.520
Yeah, I feel like that would offend many a Bostonite.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.260
And is it supposed to sound like the Red Sox?

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.360
That kind of sounds like it.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:43.800
There's mixing sports now?

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.600
Yeah, that must have been what it was.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.500
I think the Redcoats one is the biggest offender.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:55.360
But even then they get lazy and there's the Seattle Sonics, which they just removed Super from the team name.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.120
The Seattle Super Sonics basketball team.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.440
They just said, oh, if we get rid of Super, right?

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:05.940
At that point, just call it the New York Knacks, right?

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.460
The Knack is back, baby.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:15.640
Big time. I feel like the New York Slicks is just as close as New York Knacks, honestly.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.180
I think you could do that, though.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.780
New York Knacks, Chicago Balls, Boston Celtics.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.440
Well, that's really what they are, right?

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:25.540
Celtics, Celtics.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.040
Well, you're supposed to say Celtics, but...

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.280
Right. Yeah.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.100
Other ones, I guess I might as well mention them since we're bringing them all up.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.960
Los Angeles Breakers instead of Lakers.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.180
Which makes more sense.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:42.660
Love it. Dallas Stallions instead of Dallas Mavericks.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:44.900
That works, too.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.520
Yeah. Phoenix Wings instead of Phoenix Suns.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.140
Now, Phoenix Wings is a great name.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.920
Don't get me wrong. Okay, like, I appreciate that team name.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.740
Again, Phoenix Suns, they're like, you know, they're still doing the same thing, the Phoenix pun, right?

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.080
But, like, just different types of Phoenix.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:02.680
Okay, so this is...

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.120
Maybe it's a reference to Icarus.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:05.580
That's a reference to Phoenix.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.820
He flew with his wings too close to the sun.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:09.860
Yeah, but it's got to somehow connect to the sun.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.300
Okay. I love what Joe just did there.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.260
Wow, you're right. Like, I get that Phoenix have wings, but...

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.180
Man, the Watchmen universe is lit.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.220
I've never even read Watchmen.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:33.820
Eh, it's whatever. But the fact that the Phoenix Wings and the Los Angeles Breakers are better names than their actual real counterparts...

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:39.680
Like, the Phoenix Suns, it sounds like, oh, you know, it's really hot out all the time.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:42.600
Whereas there's no wordplay with their city name.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.440
And the Lakers, that's an old holdover from Minnesota.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.800
There's no lakes in LA.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.300
It's crazy.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.200
It's wild. But LA is in lakes.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.900
Ah. The La La's.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.520
This is a five-player full-length basketball court game.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:07.480
But a little bit of a twist on the full-length part, right?

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.060
It's not actually a full-length basketball court.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.580
The game has an interesting technical workaround for that.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:16.700
Sean, do you want to walk us through what that caveat is?

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:26.640
Yeah, so it only ever wants you to play from a perspective from half court to the net on the top of the screen.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:40.400
So anytime you cross the center line, you're going to get a little black screen and a second and a half delay where the game just flips everything around.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:48.900
It flips the color of the actual color of the court and changes the team name.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.000
And now that is the other side of the court.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.420
And it's definitely an experience.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:06.260
Yeah, I don't even know if you can possibly describe it and have it translate to just how jarring it is when you're playing.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.940
Like, it is, it is, it fades black.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:21.000
Your player that you have selected that usually has the ball just, like, zooms over to the other side, like, to the mirror side of where it would be if you were to suddenly flip it.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.660
And then it fades back in and you're going the other direction.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.280
It actually shows the warp for some players.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:32.480
Yeah. And it's not even, like, a perspective shift where, like, you see it rotate, which would probably be harder for them to do.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:38.460
But it's just, like, even more confusing because it's, like, yeah, you know in your head that, like, oh, okay, this is showing that it's rotating.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.620
But it just looks like you zoom across the court and everyone's, like, changed positions.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:47.660
Like, your brain isn't wired to recognize that as, like, oh, we're just looking from the other angle now.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:55.600
Right, but the turnover to the other half of the court is not an insignificant amount of time spent for that change either.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:59.920
It's almost like a loading screen in a console that doesn't have loads.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:05.820
It takes a significant amount of fade out and fade back in to resume action.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.080
And, you know, just think about it's almost, in some ways, it's like a stuttering action.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:23.080
And that's true, and it is hard to get around, but the alternative, and I'm sure that they could have done it this way, is it just immediately flickers and it's the other screen.

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:29.160
Like, it just redraws it, and there's no delay, and you're just as confused and disjointed.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:35.140
I think this is actually the lesser of two evils if they wanted to actually play it this way.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:38.000
I'd have to see it the other way.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:44.800
It does sound like this would be the lesser of two evils, but I assume that the reason for what they did here,

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:50.120
like letting it fade out and having you see your player move to the other side,

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:54.680
is so you can have that, like, split second to, like, orient and be like, okay, I'm going this way now.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:55.780
That's definitely it.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.840
I don't think that works as well in practice as it does in theory, though.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:04.640
And I almost feel like maybe if I knew it was coming every time, I could just be ready to keep moving.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.540
Because it takes away everyone else on the screen.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.740
So I know where I am, but I can't, I can't, my brain can't place everybody else on the screen where they would be.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.600
So, like, as soon as they pop up, I'm like, oh, all right, well, they stole the ball because I didn't know they were right in front of me,

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:20.580
and I ran right into them.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:26.880
Like, if it was just immediate and then I was expecting it, it would still be really, really freaking difficult, actually.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.280
So I don't know what I'm getting at here.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:31.200
But, like, it might be, it might be a little.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:40.680
I don't know if that's just, like, it would be too much for the console to handle to, like, transpose every sprite at once.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.960
Yeah. But I know exactly the feeling you're describing.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:47.060
Yeah. You know what they should have done, actually, now that I'm thinking about it?

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:49.920
It's just built the other half of the game on the other side of the court.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:56.040
Well, Joe, keep in mind, you know, we have played full-length, full basketball courts before.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:59.900
It's the perspective that is causing the issue here.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:06.380
In Double Dribble, it was a full court where you never had to worry about the loading screen on the halfway mark.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:07.800
It was both sides.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:11.400
However, that game was playing from a side perspective.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.220
So you were almost like a fan sitting in the bleachers, right?

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:27.140
Whereas in this game, because of the top-down perspective of it, I can't imagine how you would feel controlling it when the hoop is, you know, like.

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:28.240
Facing the other direction, yeah.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:33.140
Yeah, facing the other direction of which all play seems to be directed at.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:34.180
You know, there's a reason why.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:39.880
But as long as, and I get what you're saying, but as long as you can see the net.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:41.700
Well, that's the problem.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:53.620
Why is it any different? You either have, I forgot, there was a game, I forgot even what sport, but there was a sports game that had that weird, confusing perspective shift.

00:08:53.780 --> 00:09:02.220
Like where it would be the net sort of falling away from the backcourt.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:06.240
And that's how you see the net.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.920
Like it doesn't make perspective sense, but like it allows you to actually see the net and shoot.

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:13.220
It'll just be very confusing.

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:21.580
The other way they could do it is to just show the back of the net and whoever is playing on that side is going to have a hard time.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:30.960
So this is, and the other basketball games that we've played from this perspective, just, they made it just a half court game.

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:32.840
So they didn't have to worry about this.

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:39.060
So they definitely painted themselves into a corner with this problem that they had to solve.

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:46.300
Yeah. I mean, I guess I hadn't really thought about like the difference between like this and like blades of steel, which does go up and down.

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:52.200
But, but you need to see this tiny little net with the backboard, which is an important part of it as well.

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:54.080
You can't just like erase the backboard.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:10:00.380
Yeah. To, you know, I mean, but you can, you could maybe cheat it to look clear.

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:05.800
I mean, you know, and then you see through it like very clear and just like, oh, okay, there's a backboard there.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:11.300
I don't know. Maybe I'm, maybe it's easier for me to just throw these ideas out there than for someone to actually program it.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:16.220
But it feels like it would be worth, worth a shot.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:24.400
I just think, especially in top down games, like even games that aren't sports games necessarily, like in Zelda, right?

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:32.960
Like just even the way dungeons are designed or gameplay is designed, it's like, yeah, you can, you can move south, right?

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:34.460
Or downwards on the screen.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:38.180
But even the way that things are laid out, it's like, that's the exit point.

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:44.100
Like you're always entering from either like north, south, east, northeast or west after that.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:53.020
Like top down games just seem to have a more following upstream direction to them, especially in these earlier games.

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:59.620
So maybe there is just like a challenge with perspective when the bottom half of the screen has to be what you're filling in.

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:06.680
Yeah. And when you're playing a Zelda or anything like that, like the action isn't happening at that point.

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:10.880
Like whereas the net is where you're going to be focusing on.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:16.740
In Zelda, you're always sort of focusing on stuff that's in the center of the screen or near center.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:22.540
It's not, I believe it would have been a really weird experience that way too.

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:26.400
So this is five player basketball.

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:29.860
We've had double dribble, which was three on three.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:32.480
Jordan versus bird, which was one on one.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:36.600
And hoops, which had a one on one mode, but also had a two on two mode.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.100
So we have never experienced like a doubles really.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:43.920
This is five players is what you're supposed to have in basketball.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:45.740
It didn't bother us at all in double dribble.

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:51.360
If anything, it was fine because of the 2D elements of the game.

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:55.800
More more players might have been bad for for the way that the game was playing out.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:57.560
Five players here.

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:04.600
Does it make a noticeable difference in in strategy or in realism or in like?

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:12.060
I guess I'm saying like, did you utilize the extra players any more than you would have in say double dribble?

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:20.420
So no. So in a like if I'm just looking at screenshots of a game like, oh, yeah, that looks a lot more realistic.

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:22.260
It's actually five on five basketball.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:26.900
And in any like one little moment, that's true.

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:34.940
But that like for the game to actually feel like the five on five was necessary.

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:40.820
Like I would want to see like an actual like strategy for basketball.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:46.840
Whereas in this, it's just a bigger crowd to pass through and more opportunities to get stolen from.

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:51.800
So there's more hazards for sure, but it's they're not playing basketball.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.240
There's no picks and rolls.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:54.900
There's no box outs.

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:56.900
It's just they're chasing you.

00:12:57.020 --> 00:13:02.220
And there's some defensemen that are staying behind to like just get in your way.

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:06.920
Maybe this isn't necessarily an issue with the five on five nature of it.

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:12.760
But but it did seem to lend to add to my confusion a little more.

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:17.600
But there's just so much going on on the screen, especially not to keep coming back to this perspective shift.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:23.600
But that made if there were if there were just, you know, six players on the court at any time when that happened,

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:27.980
might be a little easier for me to kind of get my head around it when we when we switch to this other side of the court.

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:31.760
But when I first started, well, I think it was my second game.

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:42.760
I played the Slicks versus the Breakers and and their their jerseys are like one of them has like a has like a orangish red uniform.

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:44.980
And the other one has like a brownish red uniform.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.460
So I was very confused.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:54.580
And there's like a tiny little like same color as both uniforms one above the head of the player that you're controlling or arrow rather.

00:13:54.580 --> 00:14:01.360
And I'm just like, I'm like, I don't know who I am or where I am or what like a lot of that got very confusing.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:09.200
And I think if you thin to the herd of like how many how many players are on there a little bit, then that would that would maybe help.

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:18.540
If you're on the slick side of the court and you're playing as them, you've got your player in red on a background of red.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:22.400
You've got the red one above your head to make sure you know who you are.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:30.020
And then any the player that you're designated to pass to at any time has that red arrow pointing down.

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:31.480
So all you see is red.

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:33.720
And this is exactly what you were saying.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:42.060
But it's impossible to play that way with with just when every piece of information just blurs together.

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:44.940
I like I was passing to no one.

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:48.600
I was like trying to throw from half court.

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:51.400
It was it was a very disorienting experience.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:15:01.600
Yeah, if anything, the momentum of the gameplay relies on just you figuring out where you were once the fade out stops.

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:04.160
And then just kind of rushing the net.

00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:08.420
You're not thinking about like, where's my team on the on the court?

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:09.820
Like, who should I who's open?

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:12.040
It's like there's not really like much time for that.

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:18.760
You know, you might as well just keep trying to get the slam dunk like slam dunks are the way to score points in this game.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:22.580
Three pointers are, you know, are challenging probably on purpose.

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:24.460
They're not impossible. They can be pulled off.

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:33.940
But for the most part, it's just you in the computer rushing for possession of the ball and then immediately just making sure like who's blocking my path to the net.

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:37.860
And most likely the five players on your team and the five players on their team.

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:51.040
Most of them are doing very inconsequential things that might matter in a game where momentum wasn't so quick, where like scoring points wasn't happening so fast and play wasn't shifting so often.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:16:00.960
Yeah. And like that, I was kind of complaining about the AI earlier, but there is some like rudimentary like teammate AI.

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:06.040
Like if you're rushing the net, like someone else will be there with you on the break.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:13.980
So if you wanted to pass, even though you have to kind of just stop where you're going and then pass, you can't like pass in stride.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:17.460
You can either pass or jump pass.

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:28.320
So there's some of that like there is an outlet usually if you're being blocked entirely, but you've got an open guy.

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:32.360
But other than that, it's just sort of a crowd.

00:16:33.940 --> 00:16:40.500
And then it's also just fighting against knowing where you are, like you were saying, Mike.

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:41.420
Thank you.

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:27.440
I think defense turns out to be the biggest problem, maybe not just in NES basketball games, but in sports games in general on the NES.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:32.940
And I don't think anything's really being solved here that, you know, like I was like, oh yeah, defense feels great in this game.

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:40.520
The options they give you for defense though, both a like standing block and a jumping block.

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:45.100
I don't know. They felt more responsive than I was expecting for this kind of game.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:51.680
I wasn't, I wasn't able to pull off steals as often as I would have liked or, you know, block shots successfully.

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:56.940
But it felt responsive and it felt like I understood what those actions were meant to do.

00:17:57.040 --> 00:18:06.360
And that's not high praise or anything, but in a lot of these basketball games, it does kind of feel like you're just mashing the button while you're next to the player and hoping that you steal the ball.

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:11.000
Whereas this one felt like it was showing me what I needed to do a little more clearly.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:12.380
So I'll give it that kind of praise.

00:18:12.580 --> 00:18:13.860
Yeah, I agree.

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:26.480
Whereas I feel in other games, like there's a lot more stickiness to defensive inputs where you, like your timing has to be impeccable and you don't really know what the timing is.

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:33.500
Whereas when you press the defensive posture button, it immediately does it.

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:35.840
It doesn't matter if you've got another input pressed or not.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:38.140
So that's good.

00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:42.320
But I had like maybe a one in 10 success rate when trying to steal.

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:46.280
Whereas I feel like the CPU had a much higher success rate.

00:18:46.940 --> 00:19:03.500
But when it comes to the jump block, I feel like the only instance where that was ever taken into account was when you're rushing to the net to defend against like a layup or a dunk.

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:13.780
Like if you're jump blocking at the moment, the CPU is tossing up like a three pointer, like there's no way you're interacting with that.

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:15.920
It's not, you're not going to steal it out of the air.

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:20.740
You're not going to block it from, from that three point line.

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:24.740
It's going to either make it into the net or bounce off the backboard.

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:26.320
Like you just, you know what I mean?

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:30.520
Like there's, there's very limited interaction from what I experienced.

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:36.240
Yeah. I, sometimes I feel like, I feel like, uh, it seems like you should be blocking it.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:39.620
Like it feels like the ball goes right through you sometimes when you're trying to block.

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:51.380
And, you know, on the opposite side on shooting, um, I guess first talk about the slam dunk since we were saying that's the, uh, maybe the most used path to scoring in this game.

00:19:51.380 --> 00:20:01.560
Uh, the coolest part obviously are the slam dunk animations, but when you play the NES chronologically, like we do, it's kind of a, a tired trick at this point.

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:06.000
A lot of basketball games seem to do this, uh, zoom in on the slam dunk.

00:20:06.100 --> 00:20:12.920
And what was once maybe a, a neat novelty is now like, am I supposed to be going back to the old games and seeing like who did the cut scene better?

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:14.400
Like who made it cooler?

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:15.960
Like, cause I, I don't really care.

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:17.760
Um, it's nice.

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:23.460
Uh, I don't, I actually feel like it's the other thing that disrupts pace of play in this game.

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:25.980
Um, granted there's animation on it, right?

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:31.140
And, uh, there is both a, a win and fail state for your slam dunk.

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:32.820
There is a chance that it won't go in.

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:34.380
So that's nice. Not a guaranteed.

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:35.560
There's two win states.

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:42.200
Oh, okay. Well, like two win animations, you know, there's one where, where there's someone attempting to block and failing.

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:44.660
There's one where there's no one there.

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:46.240
And then there's one where someone actually blocks.

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:48.040
Right. Okay. That makes, yes, that's true.

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:59.600
Um, I guess my, my point being that, you know, it's nice that they're animated and that they show what might've been happening on the action screen, but it just disrupts the, uh, you know, and this is, this is crazy.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:00.700
This is what I was talking about with pace of play.

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:11.320
This game moves very quickly, but it spends most of its time in pause modes between cut scene animations and fading in and fading out at the half court line.

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:22.820
So that whenever you're actually doing anything, you're maybe doing conscious button actions for like two to three seconds before you're then like interrupted with either a fade out or a cut scene.

00:21:22.820 --> 00:21:38.240
Yeah. And then like, there'll be times where, uh, the computer opponent, if you're playing against the computer, uh, they'll cross the half court line and then whatever their code to, to advance to the net just gets broken for a second.

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:43.020
And they'll just walk back and forth across, like across that line.

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:46.560
And I just kept, you know, taking defensive posture.

00:21:46.780 --> 00:21:48.060
I'll, I'll steal it eventually.

00:21:48.340 --> 00:21:53.480
No, they just, they break out event like after a full 25 seconds.

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:58.060
So a lot of it is waiting to, for the computer just to score.

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:02.380
But it does feel satisfying when you block the slam dunk.

00:22:02.380 --> 00:22:10.480
Yeah. When that specific animation where that's, it's again, taking you out of gameplay when that happens, like that, that feels good.

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:15.920
But I, it's more satisfying to me to actually steal within the actual game.

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:17.480
Right, right.

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:23.500
Um, and then the other thing I want to talk about, uh, offense jump and shoot being the same button.

00:22:23.780 --> 00:22:30.120
Uh, I feel like this isn't the first game we've seen this in, but we're, we're talking about a is a dedicated pass button.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:43.320
So, uh, if you press the B button to jump while you're on your arc, right, you must then push the B button again at a, at a different point to launch the ball out of your hands and towards the net.

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:46.560
If you push the A button, you will just wind up passing it instead.

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:49.040
Uh, if you don't do anything, you're traveling.

00:22:49.280 --> 00:22:50.680
So, uh, that's no good.

00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:57.260
It takes like, I feel like there's something that happens to you at the beginning of the game that you, you probably just like notice and be like, oh, that's annoying.

00:22:57.500 --> 00:22:59.700
You'll, you'll get better at not doing it.

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:06.240
But I wrote it in my show notes because it was something that happened to me pretty often early on in my gameplay, uh, of all pro basketball.

00:23:06.940 --> 00:23:08.240
I'm not sure what they would have done though.

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:11.220
Like a kind of does need to be the pass button.

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:15.160
So if there is going to be a jump, it also needs to be the shoot, right?

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:17.240
Like a can't be both pass and shoot.

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:26.060
Well, this is actually one of the most, uh, in addition to the perspective, one of the most modern parts of this game.

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:31.820
Like, like I, I never really thought about it as a jump and shoot button.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:34.560
I just thought of it as the shoot button.

00:23:34.780 --> 00:23:40.560
Um, because there should never be a point where you're shooting flat footed.

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:53.160
Uh, so in, in any modern basketball game, like you're going to start each time you want to shoot, you're going to jump first and then you're going to like have that second press for where you want to release.

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:57.920
So that, that felt more natural to me than I guess it did for you.

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:16.520
Well, compared to, I think, I think it was a basketball game that I've talked about this before where it's like in an, in a rudimentary compared to today's standards video game about basketball or some other sports too, where it's like basketball, particularly you're not aiming the ball.

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:25.860
So I always felt like, at what point does it feel like it's your skill or it's just luck if the ball, if the ball, you know, it's just one button press.

00:24:25.860 --> 00:24:28.960
It's not like you hold it down to get a harder throw or anything like that.

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:32.240
So it's like, at what point is it like, do you have control over it?

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:37.840
It's that timing. And this, yeah, this made me feel that timing a lot more.

00:24:37.980 --> 00:24:47.760
Cause it's like, it made me feel like if, if I, if I made a shot, it was because I released the ball at the right moment at the, at the, at the right time in my jump.

00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:49.860
And if I missed, it felt like I didn't.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:54.060
And it always felt like there was a way I could have done it.

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:56.760
And I, and like, I could have done it better.

00:24:57.640 --> 00:25:03.600
And if I played this enough, I will get the feel of it just like you will in like, in like any real sport, you get the feel of it.

00:25:03.700 --> 00:25:04.680
You kind of get the muscle memory.

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:12.080
So like that felt, and I'm sure it's been the case in other games we've played too, but it felt a little more obvious here.

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:26.600
No, I agree. Uh, because like going back to how we've been describing, at least how it feels to, uh, try to play defense, um, where you're, you see your input reflected on your player immediately.

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:35.400
It, this seems like the most responsive in terms of just base controls basketball game that we've played.

00:25:35.620 --> 00:25:39.760
So that's where I think that, that feeling comes from where it's actually starting to click.

00:26:06.920 --> 00:26:09.700
And there's a couple different options of, of play.

00:26:09.700 --> 00:26:12.700
